 ],
 "literal": "They do not understand indeed, friend, in the cool-drizzling morning of abounding grief, / of women with carp-like collyrium-painted eyes and heavy ear-ornaments, / in the sorrowful evening when the lamps lit by pouring ghee using hands as rafts raise grief, / those who wipe the tears falling from the weeping eyes, rising from the body-swelling joy of hosting guests because their hard-to-obtain lovers have returned!",
 "translation": "O friend! Those who wipe the tears of joy falling from our eyes—eyes like carp, painted with collyrium, of women wearing heavy ear-ornaments—as we celebrate with guests, our bodies swelling with joy because our hard-to-get lovers have returned, indeed do not understand the immense grief we felt during the sorrowful evening when the lamps lit with poured ghee raise grief, and during the morning of abounding distress when the cool drizzle sprinkles!",
 "modern": "தோழியே! அரிய காதலர் வந்துவிட்டார் என்று விருந்தினரை உபசரித்து, உடல் பருக்கும் மகிழ்ச்சியோடு எழும், கயல் போன்ற மை தீட்டிய கண்களிலிருந்து வழியும் ஆனந்தக் கண்ணீரைத் துடைப்பவர்கள், குளிர்ந்த தூறல் போடும் துன்பம் மிகுந்த காலையிலும், கைகளைக் காப்பாகக் கொண்டு நெய் ஊற்றி ஏற்றிய விளக்குகள் துயரத்தை எழுப்பும் துன்ப மாலைப் பொழுதிலும் நாம் அடைந்த துயரத்தை அறிய மாட்டார்கள்.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her friend after the hero has returned from his long journey. She is filled with immense joy, hosting guests, and tears of joy flow from her eyes. When someone (either the friend or the hero) wipes away these tears, she remarks that they do not truly understand the depth of her past suffering. She contrasts her current joy with the agonizing loneliness she felt during his absence, particularly during the cold, drizzly mornings and the sorrowful evenings when she had to light the lamps. This poem belongs to the Pālai (separation) landscape, highlighting how the memory of past separation lingers even in moments of reunion.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
